## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of event-driven AWS architectures, video processing workflows, and end-to-end data indexing, with emphasis on security, reliability, and cost optimization.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven orchestration using S3 event notifications and Step Functions
- AWS Elemental MediaConvert for multi-format video processing (MP4, GIF thumbnails)
- DynamoDB indexing with comprehensive video metadata
- Security best practices: IAM least privilege, encryption at rest and in transit, access logging
- Robust error handling: retry mechanisms, dead-letter queues, comprehensive monitoring
- Cost optimization strategies: appropriate resource sizing, storage lifecycle policies, and efficient processing patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Lambda snippet: update DynamoDB after MediaConvert completion
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const { jobId, status, output } = event.detail;

  if (status === 'COMPLETE') {
    const params = {
      TableName: process.env.VIDEOS_TABLE,
      Item: {
        videoId: output.videoId,
        sourceKey: output.inputFile,
        thumbnailKey: output.thumbnailPath,
        previewKey: output.mp4Path,
        duration: output.duration,
        encodingQuality: '1080p',
        processedAt: new Date().toISOString()
      }
    };

    await ddb.put(params).promise();
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design of an AWS AI pipeline handling privacy, scaling, and cost. Combines managed AI services (Comprehend, SageMaker) with event-driven wiring (S3, Lambda, DynamoDB).

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture across S3, Lambda, and SageMaker
- AI services integration: Comprehend for NER/sentiment, SageMaker for topic modeling
- Data privacy: PII redaction, encryption at rest/in transit, access controls
- Cost awareness: batching, cold starts, endpoint usage, and streaming vs. per-file processing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: demonstrate sequence of calls to AWS services
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function analyze(text){
  const entities = await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const sentiment = await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'topic-model', Body: JSON.stringify({text}), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return {entities, sentiment, topics: JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures and retries across Lambda, SageMaker, and DynamoDB?
- What security controls and monitoring would you add for regulatory compliance (e.g., encryption, audit logs, IAM least privilege)?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to design a scalable, multi-tenant AI moderation pipeline on AWS with policy-driven routing, data isolation, and compliant audit logging.

## Key Concepts
- SageMaker multi-model endpoints and per-tenant routing
- DynamoDB for policy storage; S3 for audit logs and artifacts
- Tenant isolation via prefixes, IAM scoping, KMS encryption
- Step Functions for orchestration, error handling, retries
- Cost controls: autoscaling, model cache, off-peak scheduling

## Code Example
